<p>A relational database keeps an application’s data in tables: customers in one table, orders in another, with values that connect them. The application asks the database to store and retrieve that data. A shop can close its website for an update and still have its customers and orders when it opens again.</p>

<p>Consider a shop selling tea and tea things. Mina places an order, then comes back next week to check it. The shop needs to remember which order is hers. Its staff need the same information when they pack it, and Mina may place another order before the first one has shipped. These are several uses of the same stored facts.</p>

<p>A relational database lets the shop keep those facts together and ask questions that cross them: which customer placed this order, which orders belong to Mina, and which customers have yet to order? Learning to read the tables is the first step towards making the database answer those questions.</p>

<h2 id="tables-and-their-connections">Tables and their connections</h2>

<p>A <strong>table</strong> holds a collection of records of the same kind. In the shop’s <code>orders</code> table, each <strong>row</strong> is one order. Each <strong>column</strong> holds one kind of fact about it. The column headings name those facts: <code>order_id</code> identifies the order, and <code>customer_id</code> identifies the customer who placed it.</p>

<p>Read order 103 across its row in Figure 1. Its two values say “order 103 was placed by customer 1”. To find that customer’s name, follow the 1 into the <code>customers</code> table. Step through the drawing, then choose order 102 and follow it in the same way.</p>

<OrderCustomer />

<p>Order 103 led to customer 1, Mina Shah. Order 102 led to customer 2, Tariq Aziz. The value travels between the tables; the row’s position does not. Moving Mina’s row to the bottom of the drawing would still leave her as customer 1.</p>

<p>The two identifiers in an order’s row do different jobs. Its <code>order_id</code> distinguishes that order from other orders. Its <code>customer_id</code> points to a customer, so it can appear in several orders. Orders 101 and 103 both carry customer 1: two orders, one Mina. Her name is stored once in <code>customers</code>, rather than copied into each order.</p>

<p>Edda Berg has a customer row, with identifier 3, but none of the orders shown carries that value. A customer can exist before placing an order. And two customers could share a name: it is their identifiers that distinguish them. To read this small database, start with what one row represents, read its column headings, then follow the customer identifier to the matching customer row.</p>

